"B. To halve the chromosome number",
"C. To maintain the chromosome number",
"D. To create genetic variation"
],
"correct_answer": "C. To maintain the chromosome number",
"explanation": "Mitosis is a process of cell division that results in two daughter cells each having the same number and kind of chromosomes as the parent nucleus, typical of ordinary tissue growth."
},
{
"question": "In which part of the chloroplast does the Calvin cycle take place?",
"options": [
"A. Thylakoid membrane",
"B. Outer membrane",
"C. Stroma",
"D. Inner membrane"
],
"correct_answer": "C. Stroma",
"explanation": "The Calvin cycle, also known as the light-independent reactions, occurs in the stroma of the chloroplast."
},
{
"question": "What is the main outcome of meiosis in terms of chromosome number?",
"options": [
"A. It doubles the chromosome number.",
"B. It maintains the original chromosome number.",
"C. It halves the chromosome number.",
"D. It results in polyploidy."
],
"correct_answer": "C. It halves the chromosome number.",
"explanation": "Meiosis is a type of cell division that reduces the number of chromosomes in the parent cell by half and produces four gamete cells, ensuring genetic diversity."
}
]
}"}

