## Single-correctness check (mandatory, before finalizing each question)
Exactly one option must be defensibly correct. Check each of the other three: could any be argued correct, partially correct, or correct under a reasonable alternate reading? If so, revise or replace it — never ship a question with two viable answers. `correct_answer` must match one option verbatim.

## Distractor quality
Build the three incorrect options from plausible, exam-realistic errors where possible: a common misconception, a near-miss calculation/sign/unit error, a mix-up with a related-but-distinct term, or a value from a related-but-different scenario in the source. Avoid distractors eliminable by guessing alone.

## Numeric and formula integrity
For any formula, number, unit, or calculation: verify sign, unit, and exponent before finalizing. This is the highest-cost mistake this task can make — a wrong number or sign tends to be memorized as correct fact.

## Self-verification (internal, do not show)
