pred_json:
{"slide_deck_markdown": "[
    {
        "slide_number": 1,
        "title": "Unveiling the Lens: How Light Bends and Forms Images",
        "bullets": [
            "Lenses are fundamental optical devices that **refract light** to form images.",
            "Understanding their behavior is crucial for applications from **eyeglasses to telescopes**.",
            "We'll explore the core principles governing **image formation** and lens properties."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 2,
        "title": "The Lens Formula: Connecting Object, Image, and Focal Length",
        "bullets": [
            "The **lens formula** mathematically relates the object distance (**u**), image distance (**v**), and focal length (**f**) of a lens.",
            "It is expressed as: **1/f = 1/v - 1/u**.",
            "By convention, the **real object distance (u)** is taken as **negative**.",
            "The **focal length (f)** is positive for a converging lens and negative for a diverging lens."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 3,
        "title": "Magnification: Sizing Up the Image",
        "bullets": [
            "**Magnification (m)** describes how much larger or smaller an image is compared to the object.",
            "It is calculated as the ratio of image distance to object distance: **m = v/u**.",
            "A magnification **greater than 1** indicates an enlarged image; **less than 1** indicates a diminished image.",
            "The sign of magnification tells us if the image is **erect (positive)** or **inverted (negative)**."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 4,
        "title": "Optical Centre: The Undeviated Path",
        "bullets": [
            "Every lens has an **optical centre**, a point through which rays of light pass without deviation.",
            "This property simplifies ray diagrams, as a ray passing through the **optical centre** continues in a straight line.",
            "Why is this specific ray undeviated, unlike others that refract? â It effectively passes through parallel faces at that point."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 5,
        "title": "Power of a Lens: Measuring Bending Ability",
        "bullets": [
            "The **power (P)** of a lens quantifies its ability to converge or diverge light rays.",
            "It is defined as the **reciprocal of the focal length**: **P = 1/f**.",
            "For power to be in **dioptres (D)**, the focal length (**f**) must be expressed in **metres**.",
            "A lens with a shorter focal length has **greater power** and bends light more strongly."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 6,
        "title": "Key Takeaways: Mastering Lens Optics",
        "bullets": [
            "The **lens formula (1/f = 1/v - 1/u)** is central to calculating image positions.",
            "**Magnification (m = v/u)** determines image size and orientation.",
            "The **optical centre** provides a crucial reference for ray tracing, as rays passing through it are **undeviated**.",
            "**Lens power (P = 1/f)**, measured in dioptres, indicates the lens's converging or diverging strength.",
            "Consistent **sign conventions** (e.g., real object u negative) are vital for accurate calculations."
        ]
    }
]"}

