pred_json:
{"slide_deck_markdown": "{
  "slide_deck": [
    {
      "slide_number": 1,
      "title": "Galvanic Cells: Harnessing Spontaneous Reactions",
      "bullets": [
        "Galvanic cells (voltaic cells) convert **chemical energy** into **electrical energy** through spontaneous redox reactions.",
        "These cells are fundamental to batteries and many electrochemical processes.",
        "The key is separating oxidation and reduction half-reactions to generate a **flow of electrons**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_number": 2,
      "title": "Anode and Cathode: Where Reactions Occur",
      "bullets": [
        "The **anode** is the electrode where **oxidation** occurs (loss of electrons).",
        "The **cathode** is the electrode where **reduction** occurs (gain of electrons).",
        "Electrons flow externally from the **anode** to the **cathode**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_number": 3,
      "title": "Calculating Cell Potential (EMF)",
      "bullets": [
        "The **standard cell potential (EÂ°cell)** measures the potential difference under standard conditions.",
        "It's calculated as: **EÂ°cell = EÂ°cathode - EÂ°anode**.",
        "A **positive EÂ°cell** indicates a **spontaneous** reaction.",
        "The **Nernst equation** relates cell potential (EMF) to **non-standard concentrations** of reactants and products."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_number": 4,
      "title": "Conclusion: Key Principles of Galvanic Cells",
      "bullets": [
        "Galvanic cells drive electricity from **spontaneous redox reactions**.",
        "**Oxidation** at the **anode** and **reduction** at the **cathode** define electron flow.",
        "Cell potential (EMF) is determined by the difference in **reduction potentials** of the half-cells.",
        "The **Nernst equation** allows for calculating EMF under varying conditions, crucial for real-world applications."
      ]
    }
  ]
}"}

