  "slide_deck": [
    {
      "slide_number": 1,
      "title": "Spotting Aldehydes: The Silver Mirror Test",
      "bullets": [
        "How do we tell the difference between similar organic molecules?",
        "Today, we'll explore a classic chemical test: **Tollens' test**.",
        "This test specifically helps us identify **aldehydes**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_number": 2,
      "title": "What Makes Aldehydes Special?",
      "bullets": [
        "Aldehydes contain a **carbonyl group** (C=O) at the **end** of a carbon chain.",
        "This unique structure makes them more **reactive** than other similar compounds.",
        "The carbon in the C=O bond is ready for **nucleophilic addition**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_number": 3,
      "title": "The Tollens' Reagent: Our Chemical Detector",
      "bullets": [
        "Tollens' reagent is a special solution containing **silver ions** (Agâº).",
        "It's prepared by mixing silver nitrate with ammonia.",
        "This reagent is the key to making aldehydes visible."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_number": 4,
      "title": "The Silver Mirror Reaction: Aldehydes Only!",
      "bullets": [
        "When an **aldehyde** is heated with Tollens' reagent, a reaction occurs.",
        "The aldehyde is **oxidized**, and the silver ions are **reduced**.",
        "This reduction forms **elemental silver** (Ag), which coats the test tube.",
        "The result is a distinctive **silver mirror** on the inside of the tube."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_number": 5,
      "title": "Conclusion: A Clear Test for Aldehydes",
      "bullets": [
        "Tollens' test provides a **visual and reliable** way to identify aldehydes.",
        "The formation of a **silver mirror** is the definitive positive result.",
        "This reaction highlights the **unique reactivity** of the aldehyde functional group."
      ]
    }
  ]
}"}

