{"slide_deck_markdown": "[
    {
        "slide_number": 1,
        "title": "What Makes Aldehydes Special?",
        "bullets": [
            "Aldehydes and ketones both contain a **carbonyl group** (C=O).",
            "The key difference is where the carbonyl is located on the carbon chain.",
            "Aldehydes have at least one **hydrogen atom** directly attached to the carbonyl carbon.",
            "Ketones have **two carbon groups** attached to the carbonyl carbon."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 2,
        "title": "The Power of Oxidation: Aldehydes React Differently",
        "bullets": [
            "Aldehydes are easily **oxidized** to carboxylic acids.",
            "This is because of the hydrogen atom on the carbonyl carbon, which can be removed.",
            "Ketones, lacking this hydrogen, are much **harder to oxidize**.",
            "This difference allows us to tell them apart using specific chemical tests."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 3,
        "title": "Tollens' Test: The 'Silver Mirror' Reaction",
        "bullets": [
            "Tollens' test is a classic way to detect the presence of **aldehydes**.",
            "It uses a solution containing **silver ions** (Ag+).",
            "When an aldehyde is present, it reduces the silver ions to **metallic silver**.",
            "This metallic silver often deposits on the inside of the test tube, forming a distinctive **'silver mirror'**."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 4,
        "title": "How Tollens' Test Works: A Closer Look",
        "bullets": [
            "The aldehyde is **oxidized** to a carboxylic acid (or its salt).",
            "The silver ions (Ag+) are **reduced** to elemental silver (Ag).",
            "Ketones **do not react** with Tollens' reagent under normal conditions.",
            "This makes the silver mirror a clear indicator of an **aldehyde**."
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 5,
        "title": "Key Takeaways: Spotting Aldehydes",
        "bullets": [
            "Aldehydes have a **hydrogen** on their carbonyl carbon, making them easily oxidizable.",
            "Tollens' test uses **silver ions** to detect this unique reactivity.",
            "A **'silver mirror'** forming in the test tube is the positive result for an aldehyde.",
            "Ketones **do not give a positive** Tollens' test, helping us distinguish them."
        ]
    }
]"}

