  {
    "slide_number": 1,
    "title": "Navigating 3D Space: Understanding Line Orientation",
    "bullets": [
      "Every line in 3D space has a unique **orientation** defined by its relationship to the coordinate axes.",
      "We use **direction cosines** (l, m, n) to quantify this orientation.",
      "These cosines represent the cosine of the angles the line makes with the **positive x, y, and z-axes**, respectively."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 2,
    "title": "The Fundamental Property of Direction Cosines",
    "bullets": [
      "The direction cosines are not independent; they are linked by a crucial identity: **lÂ² + mÂ² + nÂ² = 1**.",
      "This equation ensures that the direction cosines accurately represent a single, consistent direction in 3D space.",
      "Think of it as a **normalization condition** for the direction vector of the line."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 3,
    "title": "Defining a Line in 3D: Point and Direction",
    "bullets": [
      "A line in 3D space can be uniquely defined by a **point it passes through** (xâ, yâ, zâ) and its **direction ratios** (a, b, c).",
      "The symmetric form of a line's equation is: **(x - xâ) / a = (y - yâ) / b = (z - zâ) / c**.",
      "Here, (a, b, c) are proportional to the direction cosines (l, m, n), meaning a = kl, b = km, c = kn for some scalar k."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 4,
    "title": "Skew Lines: When Lines Don't Intersect or Parallel",
    "bullets": [
      "**Skew lines** are a unique 3D phenomenon: they are neither parallel nor do they intersect.",
      "Imagine two non-parallel roads on different elevations that never meet â that's skew.",
      "Finding the **shortest distance** between skew lines is a common problem in 3D geometry."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 5,
    "title": "Calculating Shortest Distance Between Skew Lines",
    "bullets": [
      "The shortest distance between two skew lines can be found using the **cross product** of their direction vectors.",
      "This method identifies a vector perpendicular to both lines, representing the shortest path.",
      "The magnitude of the projection of a vector connecting any two points on the lines onto this perpendicular vector gives the **shortest distance**."
    ]
  }
]"}

